          "explanation": "<p>The correct answer is<strong>&nbsp;driving.</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Key Points</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<p>The explanation is as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>The word about is a<strong>&nbsp;preposition.</strong></li>\n\t<li>The word driving is a<strong>&nbsp;gerund.</strong></li>\n\t<li>The&nbsp;<strong>&#39;-ing&#39;&nbsp;</strong>form or&nbsp;<strong>gerund</strong>&nbsp;is generally used after a<strong>&nbsp;preposition.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Hence, the&nbsp;<strong>correct sentence</strong>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<em>They complained about&nbsp;<strong>driving&nbsp;</strong>in rush hour.</em></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<p><strong>Prepositions:</strong></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>A preposition usually precedes&nbsp;<strong>a noun or a pronoun.</strong></li>\n\t<li>A gerund is the<strong>&nbsp;&ndash;ing form</strong>&nbsp;of a verb that functions the same as a noun.&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>Some commonly used&nbsp;<strong>prepositions</strong>&nbsp;are:\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li>above, across, against, along, among, around, at, etc.</li>\n\t\t<li>before, behind, below, beneath, beside, between, by, etc.</li>\n\t\t<li>down, from, in, into, near, of, off, on, to, etc.</li>\n\t\t<li>toward, under, upon, with, and within, etc.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>",
          "topic": "Test"
        },
        {
          "id": "6229e1c279eb2601a00a5fb5",
          "questionNumber": 9,
          "type": "mcq",
          "text": "<p><span style=\"font-weight: 700; \">Directions: The given question has one blank indicating that something has been omitted. Choose the word for the given options that could fit in the blank correctly.</span></p>\n\n<div>You&#39;re a ______ interesting person.</div>",
          "image": null,
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "1",
              "label": "1",
              "text": "Very",
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "2",
              "label": "2",
              "text": "Too",
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "3",
              "label": "3",
              "text": "So",
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "4",
              "label": "4",
              "text": "Much",
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "5",
              "label": "5",
              "text": "Many",
              "image": null
            }
          ],
          "correctOptionId": "1",
          "marks": 0.5,
          "negativeMarks": 0.125,
          "explanation": "<p style=\"\">The correct answer is &#39;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">very&#39;</span>.</p>\n\n<p style=\"\"><span style=\"display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center;\"><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Key Points</u></span></strong></span></span></p>\n\n<ul style=\"\">\n\t<li><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Very</span>:&nbsp;We use \"<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">very</span>\" to show there is&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">a higher degree of some quality</span>. It often appears in sentences with<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">&nbsp;a positive meaning</span>, also it is used with the&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">present participle.</span>\n\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Ex</span>: That is a&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">very&nbsp;</span>good movie,&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">very&nbsp;</span>daring.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Too</span>:&nbsp;On the other hand, \"<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">too</span>\" means there is more of the quality than you want. This shows a<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">&nbsp;negative idea</span>.\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Ex</span>: That movie is&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">too&nbsp;</span>violent for me.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Much</span>:&nbsp;\"Much\" is used with<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">&nbsp;a comparative degree</span>&nbsp;also with<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">&nbsp;the past participle.</span>\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Ex</span>:&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Much&nbsp;</span>surprised,&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">much&nbsp;</span>needed.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li>Here in the sentence, as we know that the word&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">interesting&nbsp;</span>is acts as a<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">&nbsp;present participle so&nbsp;</span>we need to use<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">&nbsp;the adverb very</span>&nbsp;before it.</li>\n\t<li>Hence,&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">option 1</span>&nbsp;is the correct answer.<br />\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"\"><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">The correct sentence i</span>s:<em>&nbsp;<span style=\"\">You&#39;re a <strong>very </strong>interesting person.</span></em></p>\n\n<p style=\"\"><span style=\"display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center;\"><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span>&nbsp;</span></p>\n\n<p style=\"\"><u><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Difference between \"very\" and \" too\"&nbsp;</span></u></p>\n\n<ul style=\"\">\n\t<li>The main difference between \"<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">very</span>\" and \"<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">too</span>\" is that using \"<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">too</span>\" suggests that there is some problem.&nbsp;<em>On a really hard day, you might come home and say:</em>\n\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li>I am&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">too</span>&nbsp;tired to eat, so we should not go out for dinner.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li>On a better day, you might say:\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li>I am&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">very&nbsp;</span>tired, but I can go out for dinner.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>",
          "topic": "Test"
        },
        {
          "id": "60f580c88a8dfd75ccfb6456",
          "questionNumber": 10,
          "type": "mcq",
          "text": "<p><strong>Fill in the blanks with the correct option.</strong></p>\n\n<p>It is the time when they _______ preparing for their final performance as they all decided to win&nbsp;this competition at any cost.</p>",
          "image": null,
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "1",
              "label": "1",
              "text": "started",
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "2",
              "label": "2",
              "text": "have started",
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "3",
              "label": "3",
              "text": "has started",
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "4",
              "label": "4",
              "text": "is starting",
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "5",
              "label": "5",
              "text": "was started",
              "image": null
            }
          ],
          "correctOptionId": "1",
          "marks": 0.5,
          "negativeMarks": 0.125,
          "explanation": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>o</strong><strong>ption 1 i.e. started.</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Key Points</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Whenever we use the word-&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">it is time</span>, the&nbsp;sentence is in the&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">past tense.&nbsp;</span></li>\n\t<li>We use &#39;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">it is time</span>&nbsp;to express a present moment. In the above sentence,&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">&#39;it is time&#39;</span>&nbsp;is used to tell that subject has started preparing&nbsp;as it is time for their performance.</li>\n\t<li>The structure is-&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">it is time + subject + past verb form.</span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Past&nbsp;tense</span>&nbsp;is used to indicate that an action is completed.\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><em><span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">Example</span>:&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">It is time</span>&nbsp;the Indian team&nbsp;<span style=\"font-weight: 700;\">started</span>&nbsp;winning matches.</em></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>​Correct sentence-<em>&nbsp;</em></strong><em>It is the time when they <strong>started </strong>preparing for their final performance&nbsp;</em>as they all decided to win&nbsp;this competition at any cost.<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-62d8dc14-7fff-ba4a-1bac-9b4e5ea757e0\"><span style=\"  background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"border:none;display:inline-block;overflow:hidden;width:20px;height:20px;\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/NrgPjv3pok5EMtHAQUkp1j280dIK8nFlSQOuqf4-x5sZt8BoPjKewQTh3fxMHLX5WaVyJp99lUPZHdzzMCwxS9b2qmQHatpk8HOjCii947xJo-vFeSAPNJu6-LHK57kuNcHwGwuY\" style=\"margin-left:0px;margin-top:0px;\" width=\"20\" /></span></span><span style=\"  background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;\"> </span></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Simple past tense is used when the subject is narrating about the recent past.</li>\n\t<li>Perfect past tense is used when the subject is narrating about things that have not happened in the recent past.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/hint-text-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Hint</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>In the given sentence <strong>it is time&nbsp;</strong>is the hint which shows that subject is narrating about the recent past.</li>\n</ul>",
